{"author":{"id":"746a4d30afc20b78119cb8ed7c194f988174fc252f0e9d9eb92022244ef86db3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1781917292,"nonce":"t7X2gzF0xbupzDOznV+ikgWEqw4=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABGGkJ5A","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/301","origin":"github"},"title":"Operator extra-cameras rqt grid SIGSEGVs — NVDEC hevc_cuvid session exhaustion (de-dup double-decode, not all-CPU)","message":"## Opening the extra-cameras rqt grid SIGSEGVs (NVDEC session exhaustion)\n\n**Field evidence (2026-06-18, salmon, deployment #299):** baseline operator stack is stable, but opening the **extra rqt camera grid** hard-crashes (`rqt_gui` exit code **-11 = SIGSEGV**), which cascades SIGTERM to the UI launch group (CAMP dies as a victim). Reproducible; worked around live by keeping the extra grid closed.\n\n## Mechanism\n`bizzyboat_operator_camera_grid.yaml` decodes 4 OAK hevc streams (oak port/forward/starboard/aft `image_raw`) via **`hevc_cuvid`** (NVDEC). The **main `bizzyboat` perspective already decodes those same 4 streams**, so opening extra-cameras **doubles to ~8 concurrent `hevc_cuvid` NVDEC sessions** on the RTX 3070 Laptop → exceeds the GPU's concurrent-decode-session limit → segfault inside `hevc_cuvid`. GPU *capacity* is fine (VRAM ~2.4/8 GB, decoder util low) — it's the **session count**, not memory.\n\n## Why \"just check return codes\" doesn't fix it\nRead `ffmpeg_encoder_decoder` `decoder.cpp` (apt binary 3.0.x): the API boundary is **already defensive** — `avcodec_open2` checked (throws on fail), `av_hwdevice_ctx_create` checked (NULL → software-fallback path), `avcodec_send_packet`/`receiveFrame` returns checked. The crash is **below the return-code layer**: the CUDA *context* creates fine, but the NVDEC *decode session* is allocated lazily inside `hevc_cuvid` during `send_packet`, and over the session limit **libnvcuvid/libavcodec segfaults mid-call** rather than returning an error. No error code to check → existing guards can't catch it, and it is NOT a missing-return-check bug.\n\n## Fix options (in preference order — none require blanket software decode)\n1. **De-duplicate the double-decode (preferred):** the main grid and extra-cameras grid open *separate* cuvid sessions for the *same* 4 OAK streams. Share one decode per stream (decode once, display in both) → stays at 4 sessions → full GPU decode, zero CPU cost, no crash. Camera-grid / rqt-config change.\n2. **Cap GPU sessions, overflow to software (per-stream):** set only the overflow streams to software `hevc` via the `FFMPEGSubscriber` decoder map (currently unconfigured — recurring `no decoders configured ... defaulting to hevc_cuvid` WARN). GPU for the first N, CPU only for the rest.\n\n(Upstream `ffmpeg_encoder_decoder` could add a true \"hardware-decode-died → re-init in software\" guard, but since the failure is a SIGSEGV not a return, that's a non-trivial upstream feature, not a quick check.)\n\n## RCA step before implementing\nCapture a real backtrace: clear the stale apport dump (`sudo rm /var/crash/_opt_ros_jazzy_lib_rqt_gui_rqt_gui.1001.*`) then reopen extra-cameras once (or under gdb) to confirm the segfault is in `libnvcuvid`/`libavcodec` (driver-side, as expected) vs the app.\n\nNote: surfaced *after* the zenoh→Fast DDS switch (`477dcc1`) unmasked it — distinct from the (now-resolved) zenoh liveliness-keyexpr SIGABRT crashes.\n\n---\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context)`","files":null}]}